# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# LeakSanitizer suppressions for running the H3.NET.Native managed soak with an
# AddressSanitizer-instrumented libh3 loaded by the .NET host (see the asan-linux
# CI job). Because libasan is LD_PRELOADed, ASan/LSan track EVERY allocation in
# the process -- not just libh3's -- so the .NET runtime, ICU, and the glibc
# dynamic loader each report their own process-lifetime allocations at exit.
# Those are runtime noise, not binding bugs; this file filters them out.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS IS SAFE (does not hide real libh3 leaks)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# * LSan matching: a leak is suppressed if ANY frame in its recorded allocation
#   stack matches an entry below (substring match against the frame's module
#   path or demangled function name).
# * ASan records only the top `malloc_context_size` frames (default 30). A leak
#   originating in libh3 has the libh3 call site as its TOP frame(s) -- the
#   [LibraryImport("h3")] transition is a JIT-generated stub (anonymous memory),
#   NOT a libcoreclr.so text address -- so libh3 leaks surface with `libh3.so`
#   on top and reach the CI reporter. `libh3` is intentionally absent below, so
#   any leak whose stack contains a libh3 frame is REPORTED and fails the gate.
# * The runtime's own leaks (allocated by coreclr/JIT/ICU/loader for structures
#   held for the whole process lifetime) surface with those modules on top and
#   are the only thing matched here.
# * Buffer overflow / use-after-free / double-free are ASan ERRORS, not leaks:
#   they are NOT suppressible by this file and always fail with exitcode=1. This
#   file only tunes the LEAK half of the gate.
#
# Keep ASan's malloc_context_size at its default (do NOT raise it): a larger
# context could capture the shared thread-startup frames in libcoreclr.so at the
# base of a libh3 leak's stack and over-suppress it.

# --- .NET runtime native components ----------------------------------------
# CoreCLR, the JIT, and the host shims allocate EE/GC bookkeeping, JIT'd-code
# metadata, and host-resolution buffers once and hold them until process exit.
leak:libcoreclr.so
leak:libclrjit.so
leak:libclrgc.so
leak:libhostfxr.so
leak:libhostpolicy.so
leak:libnethost.so

# PAL / interop shims: environment, globalization, and crypto interop keep
# process-lifetime caches. libh3 never routes allocations through these.
leak:libSystem.Native.so
leak:libSystem.Globalization.Native.so
leak:libSystem.Security.Cryptography.Native

# --- ICU -------------------------------------------------------------------
# ICU (used by System.Globalization) initializes immutable data tables and
# per-converter caches that live for the process lifetime and are never freed.
# `libicu` covers libicuuc / libicui18n / libicudata / libicutu; the function
# fallbacks catch ICU statically linked into the globalization shim.
leak:libicu
leak:ucln_
leak:icudt

# --- glibc dynamic loader / TLS --------------------------------------------
# The loader retains metadata for every dlopen'd shared object, and thread-local
# storage blocks (dtv) persist for the life of each thread. These are matched by
# loader/TLS function names, which never appear in a steady-state libh3 malloc
# stack (libh3's dlopen happens once at startup, not in the hot path).
leak:_dl_
leak:__tls_get_addr
leak:allocate_dtv

# --- .NET ReadyToRun (R2R) runtime noise ------------------------------------
# Crossgen'd framework assemblies execute NATIVE R2R code mapped from the .dll
# itself, so LSan symbolizes those frames under the ASSEMBLY name (e.g.
# "System.Private.CoreLib.dll"), not libcoreclr.so - the native-module entries
# above do not match them. Seen live on CI (asan-linux round 2): one ~72 KB
# process-lifetime allocation in the vstest CONSOLE process (which never loads
# libh3) from System.Diagnostics.StackTrace materializing an exception stack
# trace through System.Reflection.Metadata's lazily-allocated PDB/metadata
# reader cache. The two metadata entries are safe under any-frame matching:
# those assemblies only appear ABOVE allocations they themselves make - never
# in a libh3 allocation stack. vstest.console.dll exists only in the runner
# process, which never loads libh3, so it cannot mask a binding leak either.
# Deliberately NOT suppressed: System.Private.CoreLib (its R2R frames could
# plausibly sit within the recorded context of a genuine libh3 leak stack).
leak:System.Reflection.Metadata
leak:System.Diagnostics.StackTrace
leak:vstest.console
